@page "/privacy"

@using Microsoft.Extensions.Options
@using Spellbound.Web.Hosting

@inject IOptions<PrivacyOptions> Options
@inject IConfiguration Configuration

<PageTitle>Privacy</PageTitle>

<h1>Privacy</h1>

<p>
    Spellbound is run by <b>@Show(Notice.Operator, "the operator")</b>, who decides what happens to your data. Questions
    or requests: <b>@Show(Notice.Contact, "ask the game's admin")</b>.
</p>

<h2 class="h5">What the game stores, and why</h2>
<ul>
    <li>
        <b>Your account:</b> the email address and the account id Google gives when you sign in, and when you joined and
        last signed in. To sign you in and to recognise the game's admins. The game sends no email.
    </li>
    <li>
        <b>Your sign-in networks:</b> a keyed hash of the internet address you sign in from (it can't be turned back into
        the address), kept for 30 days. To spot one person playing several accounts.
    </li>
    <li>
        <b>Your game:</b> your mages in every round, their news, and every action you take with its result and your numbers
        afterwards. To run the game, settle disputes and balance it. Kept until you delete your account. Halls of fame
        of finished rounds keep your mage's name and numbers, and other players' records of battles with you (their
        news and their action log) keep your mage's name and the battle's numbers, without your account.
        In Skirmish games: your seat, the orders you save for each round, and a record of every round played (everyone's
        orders, the luck drawn and what happened). If you delete your account, your seat stays in its games under its
        mage name without your account, so the others can finish.
    </li>
    <li>
        <b>Bans:</b> if you're banned, the reason, when, and which admin. Kept even after your account is deleted, so a
        ban can't be shed that way.
    </li>
</ul>

<h2 class="h5">Cookies</h2>
<p>
    A sign-in cookie (30 days, renewed while you play), a short one while you sign in with Google, and one that protects
    forms. No ads, no analytics, no tracking.
</p>

<h2 class="h5">Who else sees it</h2>
<ul>
    <li><b>Other players</b> see your mage: its name, school, land and networth, and the reports of battles with you.
        In a Skirmish, the players in that game see your regions, buildings, army, points and whether you're ready, but
        not your gold, mana or orders.</li>
    <li><b>The game's admins</b> see your email, your mages, which accounts share a network, and the action log.</li>
    <li><b>Google</b> handles your sign-in, under Google's own privacy policy.</li>
    <li><b>The hosting provider</b> runs the server: @Show(Notice.Hosting, "a rented server").</li>
</ul>
<p>Nothing is sold or shared with anyone else. The server doesn't log internet addresses.</p>

<h2 class="h5">Backups</h2>
<p>
    The database is backed up every night, and the server keeps the last @BackupsKept
    backups@(Notice.OffsiteBackups is { Length: > 0 } offsite ? $"; copies are also kept {offsite}" : "").
    Data you delete disappears from the backups as they're replaced.
</p>

<h2 class="h5">Your choices</h2>
<ul>
    <li><b>Get a copy</b> of everything stored about you: <a href="Account/Manage">Account</a> → Download my data.</li>
    <li><b>Delete your account</b> and your game data at once: <a href="Account/Manage">Account</a> → Delete my account.</li>
    <li><b>Ask</b> the operator anything above, or to correct something.</li>
    <li>If you're unhappy with the answer, you can complain to the data protection authority in your country.</li>
</ul>

@code {
    private PrivacyOptions Notice => Options.Value;

    private int BackupsKept => Math.Max(1, Configuration.GetValue("Backup:Keep", 7)); // as BackupKeeper reads it

    private static string Show(string? value, string otherwise) => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(value) ? otherwise : value;
}
